# Line endings — strict LF normalisation.
#
# Rationale: every container in this project is Linux, including under Docker
# Desktop for Windows. A file written with CRLF and mounted into a container
# fails opaquely ("bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" on a script, broken parsing on
# a configuration file).
#
# The rule below stores everything as LF in history *and* checks it out as LF,
# whatever the contributor's platform. The repository therefore behaves
# identically on Windows, macOS and Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files: no conversion, on pain of corruption.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.gif  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.parquet binary

# Versioned open-data snapshots: stored byte for byte, never normalised.
#
# The rule above turns every text file into LF on checkout. Applied to these
# files it would rewrite the CRLF line endings the provider actually serves,
# and the committed file would no longer match the SHA-256 published beside it
# — which is the whole point of a verifiable snapshot.
#
# -text disables end-of-line conversion while keeping the files diffable and
# viewable on GitHub, unlike the stronger `binary`.
data/snapshots/*.csv    -text
data/snapshots/*.sha256 -text
